{
  "id": "ep215",
  "duration": 47.217,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.988,
      "text": "Persistent myths claim Earth blocks the Sun to change the Moon's shape.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Full disc of the moon was photographed by the Apollo 17 crewmen.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Full_disc_of_the_moon_was_photographed_by_the_Apollo_17_crewmen.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.0,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.988,
      "end": 9.858,
      "text": "This cycle has been tracked for nearly 10,000 years at the Warren Field site in Scotland.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.0,
          "end": 9.867,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 9.858,
      "end": 15.953,
      "text": "The ancient pit alignment beats any other formal record of human timekeeping ever discovered.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 9.867,
          "end": 15.967,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 15.953,
      "end": 23.048,
      "text": "Despite these millennia of observation, many people still misunderstand the physical trick behind these phases.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Earthshine- the Moon shining in reflected light from the Earth (eso1210c).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Earthshine-_the_Moon_shining_in_reflected_light_from_the_Earth_(eso1210c).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 15.967,
          "end": 19.733,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 19.733,
          "end": 23.033,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 23.048,
      "end": 30.793,
      "text": "The Sun constantly illuminates half the Moon, but our viewpoint shifts as it orbits Earth every 27.3 days.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Popular astronomy airy(1881) page 230 figure 59.png",
        "credit": "George Biddel Airy",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Popular_astronomy_airy(1881)_page_230_figure_59.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 23.033,
          "end": 30.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.793,
      "end": 37.368,
      "text": "We aren't seeing Earth\u2019s shadow; we are watching the boundary between lunar day and night from different angles.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Art002e010032 (April 6, 2026) \u2013 The Artemis II crew captured this view near Hertzsprung and Vavilov craters along the lunar terminator, the boundary between the Moon\u2019s illuminated and shadowed regions.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Johnson Space Center",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Art002e010032_(April_6,_2026)_%E2%80%93_The_Artemis_II_crew_captured_this_view_near_Hertzsprung_and_Vavilov_craters_along_the_lunar_terminator,_the_boundary_between_the_Moon%E2%80%99s_illuminated_and_shadowed_regions.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.8,
          "end": 33.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 33.467,
          "end": 37.367,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 37.368,
      "end": 43.413,
      "text": "Outside of rare eclipses occurring twice a year, Earth's shadow never actually touches the Moon.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Total Lunar Eclipse & Blood Moon 2025 (GRC-2025-C-01613).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Glenn Research Center / NASA/Jordan Cochran",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Total_Lunar_Eclipse_%26_Blood_Moon_2025_(GRC-2025-C-01613).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 37.367,
          "end": 39.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 39.667,
          "end": 43.4,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.413,
      "end": 46.867,
      "text": "The visual illusion remains convincing to this day.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Full disc of the moon was photographed by the Apollo 17 crewmen.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Full_disc_of_the_moon_was_photographed_by_the_Apollo_17_crewmen.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.4,
          "end": 47.233,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
